# Reference verification, batch 9: s055-s061 (Nov 2026)
upd("n786", append="""[Checked: Hilary, *De Trin.* IX (PL 10, la.wikisource text of Migne). There is no *necessitas … constituit*. IX 56 has *Nativitas Filii patrem constituit majorem*. Gorris's *necessitas* may be a misreading of an abbreviated *nativitas*. This is a conjecture; the English is unchanged.]""", status="conjecture")
upd("n868", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès prints ‘QUAESTIO VI. Utrum charitas augeatur per extractionem partis novae de potentia ad actum?’ in the *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 2 block. The Wadding note cites ‘Henricus quodlib. 4, quaest. 18’. That is wrong, because *Quodl.* IV q. 18 is about St Paul; see n870. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n870", text="""Gorris identifies Scotus's anonymous *unus magister* as Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* V q. 19 (‘vtrum habitus infusus possit augeri’). This is correct. Scotus's report, ‘possibilitas ad augmentationem in forma infusa, non est accipienda ex parte subjecti … sed … ex parte formae ipsius in se, quae in sua essentia partes habet’, is nearly verbatim Henry: ‘ex parte subiecti non est possibilitas ad augmentum formae nisi sit educta de potentia materiae … Sed est accipienda ex parte ipsius formae in se scilicet quia in sua essentia partes habet’.""", append="""[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* V q. 19 (Badius 1518, via SCTA), and Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 2 (Wadding/Vivès). A's *q. 19* is right. B's *q. ix* and Wadding's ‘quodlib. 4, quaest. 18’ are wrong. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n874", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès, *Ord.* I d. 35 q. un. (‘Utrum in Deo sint relationes aeternae ad omnia scibilia ut quidditative cognita’) and the d. 36 Scholium (‘tenet esse diminutum creaturae, non esse respectivum tantum, sed etiam absolutum’). Verified for the doctrine. The rose example was not traced.]""", status="verified")
upd("n876", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès heading, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 19 > Quaestio 1: Utrum personae divinae sint aequales secundum magnitudinem’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n877", text="""Jn 14:28 (*Pater maior me est*), as expounded by Hilary, *De Trin.* IX 53–56. The passage is IX 56: ‘Major itaque Pater est, dum pater est: sed Filius, dum filius est, minor non est. Nativitas Filii patrem constituit majorem’.""", append="""[Checked: Hilary, *De Trin.* IX 53–56 (PL 10, la.wikisource text of Migne). Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n882", append="""[Checked: the Wadding/Vivès text of *Ord.* I d. 19 q. 1 has the distinction ‘aequalitatem quantitatis, non molis … sed virtutis’ and cites Augustine, *De Trin.* VI, for *quantitas virtutis*. No *De generatione* reference was found there. The Aristotelian locus was not found. Still open.]""")
upd("n885", append="""[Checked: *Metaph.* X 5 (Perseus Greek): ἀμφοῖν ἄρα ἀπόφασις στερητική (1056a15–16), and τὸ ἴσον … ἀντίκειται ἀμφοῖν ὡς ἀπόφασις στερητική (1056a22–24). Verified for the locus. Gorris's ‘4’ remains unexplained.]""", status="verified")
upd("n886", text="""Richard of Middleton, *In I Sent.* d. 19 a. 1 q. 3 (‘Utrum inter diuinas personas sit aequalitas vna numero’). He concludes ‘est ergo vna aequalitas in numero in tribus personis’, because the proximate foundation, the *quantitas virtutis*, is not numbered in the three. Scotus argues for the opposite.""", append="""[Checked: Richard of Middleton, *Super quatuor libros Sententiarum*, vol. 1 (Brescia 1591, ed. L. Silvestri; archive.org imgmar4754MiscellaneaOpal), I d. 19 a. 1 q. 3. Wadding's note to *Ord.* I d. 19 q. 1 also cites ‘Richard. art. 1. quaest. 2. et 3.’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n890", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès heading, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 19 > Quaestio 2: Utrum quaelibet persona sit in alia’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n893", text="""Henry of Ghent, *Summa quaestionum ordinariarum*, art. 53 q. 10: ‘quod in diuinis non sit vna persona in alia mutuo & eodem modo’ (the objection). Henry asks whether one person is in another by substance or by relation, and quotes Hilary, *De Trin.* III 1. Gorris gives no article number.""", append="""[Checked: Henry, *Summa* art. 53 q. 10 (Badius 1520, via SCTA). The Wadding note to *Ord.* I d. 19 q. 2 cites ‘Henr. sum. art. 1. et quodlib. 6. quaest. 5’. The *art. 1* does not fit the *Summa* question on this topic. Its *Quodl.* VI q. 5 is on Christ's glory during the triduum, so that reference does not fit. The Scholium reads ‘Sententia Henrici, unam personam esse in alia non secundum se totam, sed secundum aliquam partem sui’. Verified for the *Summa* locus.]""", status="verified")
upd("n897", append="""[Checked: Hilary, *De Trin.* III 1 (PL 10, la.wikisource): ‘Affert plerisque obscuritatem sermo Domini, cum dicit: Ego in Patre, et Pater in me’. This is quoted by Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 19, and by Henry, *Summa* art. 53 q. 10. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n898", text="""Ambrosiaster, *In 2 Cor.*, transmitted as Ambrose's, as quoted by Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 19 c. 4: ‘Per hoc intelligitur Pater esse in Filio et Filius in Patre, quia una est eorum substantia. Ibi est enim unitas, ubi nulla est diversitas’.""", append="""[Checked: Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 19 c. 4 (SCTA). The Ambrosiaster text itself (CSEL 81) was not opened. Verified via Lombard.]""", status="verified")
upd("n901", append="""[Checked: Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 19 q. 2 (Wadding/Vivès): ‘ut pes avis in laqueo, et nihil ejus est, et per hoc avis tota dicitur esse in laqueo’. Gorris has the example from Scotus. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n903", text="""Ambrose, the hymn *Splendor paternae gloriae*, last stanza: ‘Aurora cursus provehit, / aurora totus prodeat, / in Patre totus Filius, / et totus in Verbo Pater’. Gorris inverts the two half-lines.""", append="""[Checked: the hymn text (as given online, with the references Fontaine ed. 1992, p. 187, and PL 16, 1411–1412). This was found by a web search, and the published editions were not opened. Verified for the identification.]""", status="verified")
upd("n909", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès heading, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 20 > Quaestio 1: Utrum tres personae sint aequales in potentia’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n911", text="""Ps.-Augustine (Fulgentius), *De fide ad Petrum*. Compare 1.4–5. The wording is quoted via Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 19 c. 1 (not d. 20): ‘ut Augustinus in libro De fide ad Petrum … docet: “Nullus horum alium aut praecedit aeternitate, aut excedit magnitudine, aut superat potestate”’.""", append="""[Checked: Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 19 c. 1 (SCTA). The earlier reference to d. 20 has been corrected. Fulgentius's text itself (CCSL 91A) was not opened. Verified via Lombard.]""", status="verified")
upd("n914", append="""[Checked: archive.org has scans of the Junta *Aristotelis opera cum Averrois commentariis* (Venice 1562–74) for several volumes (I, II, III, IV, V, VII, IX and supplements), but no vol. VIII (Metaphysics) was found among them. The comment is not identified. Still open.]""")
upd("n915", text="""Augustine, *Contra Maximinum* II 14.7: ‘Nihil enim Patre minus habet ille qui dicit: Omnia quae habet Pater, mea sunt’ (Jn 16:15). Modern editions have two books; ‘book 3’ reflects the medieval count with the *Collatio* as book 1. Scotus (Wadding) also writes ‘Augustinus contra Maximinum lib. 3’. The text is quoted via Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 20 c. 2.""", append="""[Checked: augustinus.it Latin text of *Contra Maximinum* II, at 14.7; Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 20 cc. 1–3 (SCTA); and *Ord.* I d. 20 q. 1 (Wadding/Vivès). Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n916", text="""Giles of Rome, *In I Sent.* d. 20 q. 1 a. 2: ‘Utrum potentia generandi comprehendatur sub omnipotentia?’. Gorris's ‘q. 2’ is the article. B has the same reference, *li. j. distin. xx. q. ij.* Giles holds that ‘posse generare directe et simpliciter sub omnipotentia non continetur, indirecte autem, et quasi ex consequenti … comprehenditur’. Scotus (*Ord.* I d. 20) reports the view as ‘potentia generandi pertinet ad omnipotentiam in Patre, non in Filio’, and Wadding's Scholium names Giles. Gorris's summary follows Scotus's report rather than Giles's own nuanced wording.""", append="""[Checked: Giles, *Commentarium in primum librum Sententiarum* (Córdoba 1699; archive.org A218073, OCR), d. 20 q. 1 a. 2, and *Ord.* I d. 20 q. 1 (Wadding/Vivès, with Scholium). Probable: the article is right, but the doctrine is reported loosely.]""", status="probable")
upd("n917", append="""[Checked: *Ord.* I d. 20 q. 1 (Wadding/Vivès). Scotus refutes the view that generating belongs to the Father's omnipotence but not the Son's: ‘ergo carens tali potentia ut Filius, non habet omnipotentiam absolute’. Gorris's thesis is Scotus's conclusion. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n920", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès heading, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 21 > Quaestio 1: Utrum haec sit vera “solus Pater est Deus”’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n921", text="""Augustine, *De Trin.* VI 9.10: ‘Et quoniam ostendimus quomodo possit dici solus Pater … consideranda est illa sententia, qua dicitur: Deum verum solum, non esse Patrem solum sed Patrem et Filium et Spiritum Sanctum’. Gorris's ‘c. 8’ reflects an older chapter division. The passage is quoted by Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 21 c. 2.""", append="""[Checked: augustinus.it Latin text of *De Trin.* VI, at 9.10, and Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 21 c. 2 (SCTA), which has ‘in VI libro De Trinitate’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n922", text="""Priscian, *Institutiones grammaticae* XVIII (the second book *De constructione*). Scotus gives the dictum: ‘adducentes illud Prisciani secundo constructionum: Inter exigens et exactum cadit aliquid medium’. Bonaventure (I d. 4 dub. 3) cites Priscian for a related point: ‘inter adiectivum et substantivum intelligitur media copula ens vel quod est ens’.""", append="""[Checked: *Ord.* I d. 21 q. un. (Wadding/Vivès) and Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 4 dub. 3 (Quaracchi). The Priscian text itself was not opened. Probable.]""", status="probable")
upd("n924", text="""Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 4, *dubia circa litteram*, dub. 3. The dubium is on the Master's distinction that in *Deus Pater* the construction may be appositive and immediate, or mediate. It considers the sophism with *solum … Pater*, and answers with Priscian (‘inter adiectivum et substantivum intelligitur media copula ens vel quod est ens’).""", append="""[Checked: Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 4 dub. 3 (Quaracchi I, OCR). Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n925", text="""Giles of Rome, *In I Sent.* d. 21 q. 1 a. 1: ‘Utrum dictio exclusiva addita uni relativorum excludat reliquum?’. Giles answers that ‘relativa … sunt de intellectu secundario eorum, in quibus intelliguntur’, whereas ‘superiora sunt de intellectu primo inferiorum’. This is the view Gorris reports and rejects.""", append="""[Checked: Giles, *In I Sent.* (Córdoba 1699; archive.org A218073, OCR), d. 21 q. 1 a. 1. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n930", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès heading, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 22 > Quaestio 1: Utrum Deus sit nominabilis a nobis aliquo nomine significante essentiam divinam in se, ut est “haec”’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n933", text="""Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* V q. 1 and XIII q. 1. In V q. 1, on the plurality of attributes, the intellect ‘primo & simplici intelligentia concipit ipsam essentiam sub ratione essentiae, & deinde negotiando’ forms the rational distinctions. In XIII q. 1, on whether God can be seen *sub ratione veri* without *sub ratione boni*, the divine nature ‘obiectiue se offert & representat vt vnum simpliciter sub vna ratione simplicissima tantum, videlicet sub ratione esse’.""", append="""[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* V q. 1 and XIII q. 1 (Badius 1518, via SCTA). Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n934", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès prints for *Ord.* I d. 22 a second, supplementary question, with the Scholium ‘Haec quaestio eadem cum praecedente, sed addita est, quia copiosius rem tractat’. This bears out Gorris's remark that the text needed completing. The Vatican apparatus was not consulted. Probable.]""", status="probable")
upd("n942", append="""[Checked: Wadding/Vivès heading, ‘Liber 1 > Distinctio 23 > Quaestio 1: Utrum persona secundum quod dicit aliquid commune Patri et Filio et Spiritui Sancto, dicat praecise aliquid secundae intentionis’. Note *praecise* where Gorris has *precipue*. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n943", text="""Giles of Rome, *In I Sent.* Wadding's Scholium to *Ord.* I d. 23 also names him: ‘Sententia Aegidii (et videtur Henrici) est, personam, quia est communis tribus, esse secundam intentionem’. But Giles's printed commentary contradicts this. In the question ‘Utrum in divinis Persona sit nomen commune’ (between the running heads of dd. 24 and 25), he rejects the third position, which places the community of *persona* ‘secundum intentionem’ (as *species* is common to man and lion): ‘Persona non est nomen secundae intentionis, sicut individuum’. Gorris's attribution, which Wadding shares, may rest on another work or redaction of Giles, or may be a misattribution.""", append="""[Checked: Giles, *In I Sent.* (Córdoba 1699; archive.org A218073, OCR), dd. 24–25, and the Wadding Scholium to *Ord.* I d. 23. Uncertain.]""", status="uncertain")
